{"name":"Blue Hydrangea","hex":"#bbc3dd","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"hydrangea","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several shrubs, of the genus Hydrangea, having large clusters of white, pink or blue flowers."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hydrangea"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hydrangea","blocks":[{"text":"Hydrangea is a genus of more than 70 species of flowering plants native to Asia and the Americas. Hydrangea is also used as the common name for the genus; some are also often called hortensia. The genus was first described from Virginia in North America, but by far the greatest species diversity is in eastern Asia, notably China, Korea, and Japan. Most are shrubs 1–3 m tall, but some are small trees, and others lianas reaching up to 30 m (100 ft) by climbing up trees. They can be either deciduous or evergreen, though the widely cultivated temperate species are all deciduous."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Hydrangea_arborescens_139866012.jpg/330px-Hydrangea_arborescens_139866012.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydrangea","wikidata":"Q155997"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Hydrangea_Flower_Color_Based_on_Soil_pH.jpg/500px-Hydrangea_Flower_Color_Based_on_Soil_pH.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Hydrangea_Flower_Color_Based_on_Soil_pH.jpg/500px-Hydrangea_Flower_Color_Based_on_Soil_pH.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hydrangea flower color changes based on the pH in soil. As the graph depicts, soil with a pH of 5.5 or lower will produce blue flowers, a pH of 6.5 or higher will produce pink hydrangeas, and soil in between 5.5 and 6.5 will have purple hydrangeas.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHydrangea_Flower_Color_Based_on_Soil_pH.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Hydrangea_paniculata_-_flower_view_01.jpg/500px-Hydrangea_paniculata_-_flower_view_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Hydrangea_paniculata_-_flower_view_01.jpg/500px-Hydrangea_paniculata_-_flower_view_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hydrangea paniculata","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHydrangea_paniculata_-_flower_view_01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Hydrangea_knowltoni_as_Hydrangea_bendirei_USNM_P36979_img3.jpg/330px-Hydrangea_knowltoni_as_Hydrangea_bendirei_USNM_P36979_img3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Hydrangea_knowltoni_as_Hydrangea_bendirei_USNM_P36979_img3.jpg/330px-Hydrangea_knowltoni_as_Hydrangea_bendirei_USNM_P36979_img3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hydrangea knowltoni","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHydrangea_knowltoni_as_Hydrangea_bendirei_USNM_P36979_img3.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Hortensiapink.JPG/120px-Hortensiapink.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Hortensiapink.JPG/250px-Hortensiapink.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Hortensiapink.JPG/120px-Hortensiapink.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHortensiapink.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Hydrangea_close_up.jpg/120px-Hydrangea_close_up.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Hydrangea_close_up.jpg/250px-Hydrangea_close_up.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Hydrangea_close_up.jpg/120px-Hydrangea_close_up.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHydrangea_close_up.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thum